{
  "note": "Domain patterns that can never be an oil-spec source, whatever the searching model classifies them as. prompt.md names forums, social media, generic SEO pages, retailer descriptions and unreferenced generated content as non-authoritative. The `manual mirror` group was added after the 2026-09-10 showcase run: the searching model classified third-party manual re-hosts as `manufacturer_manual` tier 1, and only the fetch cap kept them out. A copy of a manual on someone else's ad-funded site is not the manufacturer's document, and its being a copy is invisible to a classifier reading the page. The `document sharing site` group came out of the second run, where Scribd uploads of the Argo manual were accepted as tier 1 twice: the same reasoning, and the same blind spot. The `parts shop product listing` group is different in kind: pecas.vw.com.br is the manufacturer's OWN domain, so no authority argument excludes it, but the page is a single-SKU listing for one 5W-40 bottle. prompt.md names retailer descriptions as non-authoritative, and a page whose whole subject is one product is the clearest way to make an extractor report that product's grade as the car's specification. A pattern matches the registrable domain and any subdomain of it. This list is the floor, not the ceiling: a domain absent from here still has to be classified into one of the five document classes to be accepted at all.",
